# <%= current_page.data.title %>

> This is a draft supported pattern. Development infrastructure has been deployed,
> but recipient setup and live access tests are still needed before customer use.

Use this pattern when people need to collect clean files through a browser.
Slack tells them when a file is ready and links to the existing AWS Transfer web app.
They sign in with their organisational account and download files their group can access.

## How it works

![Clean-file events reach a copy and notification function through EventBridge, SNS and SQS. Slack links to the Transfer web app; IAM Identity Center and S3 Access Grants control access to the pickup bucket.](/images/supported-patterns-pull-from-presigned-url/pull-from-presigned-url.png)

[Download the editable draw.io diagram](/images/supported-patterns-pull-from-presigned-url/pull-from-presigned-url.drawio).
Open it in draw.io with the AWS architecture shape library enabled.

1. A file passes scanning. Its event goes through Amazon EventBridge, Simple
   Notification Service (SNS) and Simple Queue Service (SQS).
2. A Lambda function copies that exact file version into a private pickup bucket.
   This leaves the clean bucket's retention unchanged.
3. After the copy succeeds, Slack receives a link to the Transfer web app and the
   bucket and path where the file is stored.
4. The recipient signs in through IAM Identity Center, finds the file and downloads it.
   S3 Access Grants restricts them to their group's pickup directory.

The Slack link opens the portal, not the individual file. It contains no signed S3 URL.
This component currently owns the copy step and pickup bucket; it does not reuse
`push-to-s3-with-hosted-pickup`.

## Who can download files

We agree a source prefix and an authorised group for each recipient. Files are stored
under that recipient's directory. Access is read-only: the group cannot upload or
delete files there, or access another recipient's directory without a separate grant.

Being a MoJ user, or having access to the Transfer web app, does not automatically
allow access to these files. Forwarding a Slack link does not grant access either.
The existing Identity Center setup is used; no separate sign-in application is needed.

## How long files are available

The pickup bucket has a seven-day lifecycle rule. S3 removes expired files
asynchronously, so this is **not an exact seven-day access or deletion deadline**.
Tell us if your service needs a strict cutoff before adopting this pattern.

The bucket rejects signed S3 requests whose signatures are more than five minutes old.
The Transfer web app manages the download links; we still need to verify its download
and retry behaviour against this limit. A signed link may be shared and reused during
its effective lifetime, and a download started before expiry can finish afterwards.

Removing group access may not immediately revoke credentials already issued to a
user. The live access tests must establish that delay before customer use.

## Arrange a trial

Contact `#ask-integration-hub` with:

- the environment and source file prefix you want to use
- the Identity Center group that should collect the files
- the Slack channel for notifications
- expected file sizes and how long users need to collect them

Do not post webhook URLs, credentials or file contents in the channel.
No recipients are enabled in the current configuration.

Team members can follow the [setup and test guide](../operations/setting-up-slack-file-pickup.html).
